    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Hungary's change from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>, the rendszerváltás, was peaceful: Round Table Talks began in March 1989, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-nagy">Imre Nagy</a> was reburied as a martyr that June, and free elections in May 1990 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozsef-antall">József Antall</a> prime minister.</li><li>Hungary joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1999, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> on 1 May 2004 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schengen-area">Schengen Area</a> in 2007; in January 2008 its border guards were merged into the police.</li><li>From 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viktor-orban">Viktor Orbán</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidesz">Fidesz</a> won four parliamentary supermajorities and passed a new constitution, in an era of democratic backsliding that ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-magyar">Péter Magyar</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza-party">Tisza Party</a> won the election of April 2026.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Hungary's Fundamental Law contains eternity clauses guaranteeing human dignity, the separation of powers, the state structure and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a>, which even the two-thirds supermajority needed for other amendments may not touch.</p></aside>
    </section>

